window.PREP_DATA = window.PREP_DATA || {};
window.PREP_DATA.aws = {
  notes: [
    { title: "What AWS is", body: "The problem before\nYou buy racks, power, and a locked room. Capacity sits idle most of the year. Opening another city means another building project.\n\nWhat this is\nAmazon Web Services is a collection of on-demand infrastructure APIs. You rent compute, storage, databases, and networking instead of owning a data center, and you pay for consumed capacity. A Region is a geography. An Availability Zone is an isolated campus inside that Region, and IAM authorizes every API call.\n\nWhat it solves\nYou open a toolbox in many cities without buying the building. You still design how services connect and fail over. The cloud is rented APIs, not a finished app.\n\nReal-life example\nA rented market in many cities: kitchens for compute, warehouses for storage, locked back rooms for networks. Mumbai is a Region. Two separate substations in that city are Availability Zones.\n\nUses\nEvery AWS interview opener. Sketch Region, AZ, and IAM before you name twenty services.\n\nWatch out\nListing service names with no request path. Forgetting that you still own IAM, data, and failover." },
    { title: "IAM first", body: "The problem before\nA leaked key can do everything. Access keys sit in a file and never expire. You cannot say who may open which door.\n\nWhat this is\nIAM answers who may call which API on which resource. Users are long-lived humans. Roles are assumed and issue temporary credentials. Policies are JSON with Effect, Action, Resource, and optional Condition; prefer roles, least privilege, and MFA.\n\nWhat it solves\nA leak has a smaller blast radius. Apps wear a day pass, not a photocopied house key. You revoke by changing a policy instead of hunting keys in git.\n\nReal-life example\nA building badge that lists which doors open. A visitor pass that expires today is safer to drop than an employee master key left in email.\n\nUses\nEvery AWS design. Interview opener after What is AWS. EC2, Lambda, and CI should assume roles.\n\nWatch out\nAction * and Resource * on a human. Committing access keys. Skipping MFA on console login." },
    { title: "EC2", body: "The problem before\nYou buy a physical box and wait weeks. Or you SSH into one named pet forever and cannot replace it. Port 22 is open to the street.\n\nWhat this is\nAmazon EC2 provides virtual machines. You pick an AMI, instance type, volumes, and security groups, and you patch the guest OS unless a higher service owns that layer. Billing continues while it runs. An Auto Scaling Group keeps a replaceable fleet from a launch template.\n\nWhat it solves\nYou rent computers by the hour and treat them as cattle. Unhealthy boxes get replaced. Place app instances behind a load balancer in private subnets. Prefer Session Manager over inbound SSH.\n\nReal-life example\nA rented kitchen stall in a locked market hall. The stall recipe is the AMI. Three identical stalls beat one famous stove you cannot rebuild.\n\nUses\nApp servers, bastions, batch workers. Interviews: AMI, security group, ASG.\n\nWatch out\nPublic SSH on port 22 to the world. Putting the only database on a single instance you dare not replace. Hand-editing a live box so the next scale-out boots the old template." },
    { title: "S3", body: "The problem before\nDisks fill up. A shared POSIX disk is hard to scale. One overwrite deletes the only copy. A public folder leaks because someone wanted the website to work.\n\nWhat this is\nAmazon S3 stores objects at keys inside buckets. It is highly durable object storage, not a Linux filesystem, and slashes in keys are naming, not directories. Keep Block Public Access on unless the bucket must serve a public site. Versioning, encryption at rest, and lifecycle rules are baseline controls.\n\nWhat it solves\nHuge files and backups have a home. You GET and PUT over HTTP APIs. Durability is the almost-never-lose-the-bytes story, which is not the same as uptime this minute.\n\nReal-life example\nA coat-check: bag in, ticket out. The ticket is the key. There is no cd into a folder even if the ticket says 2026/cat.jpg. Desk, closet, warehouse are storage classes.\n\nUses\nPhotos, backups, static sites behind CloudFront, data lakes. Interviews: bucket, key, Block Public Access.\n\nWatch out\nPublic buckets. Treating S3 like a database disk. Glacier for thumbnails the website needs on every page load." },
    { title: "VPC", body: "The problem before\nEverything sits on the public internet. The database has a public IP so you can connect from a cafe. App and data share one huge street.\n\nWhat this is\nA VPC is a customer-defined private network with a CIDR, public and private subnets, and route tables. An Internet Gateway gives bidirectional internet for public IPs. A NAT Gateway lets private instances start outbound only. Security groups are stateful firewalls; apps and databases sit private, the load balancer often public.\n\nWhat it solves\nInternal roads stay internal. The shop window can face the street. You control who talks to whom. Plan IP ranges so you can peer later.\n\nReal-life example\nA fenced campus: shop window on the street, safe in the back room. NAT is a one-way mail slot out. The bouncer on each door is a security group.\n\nUses\nEvery real workload. Interviews: public vs private, IGW vs NAT.\n\nWatch out\nOne huge public subnet for app and database. A single NAT in one AZ. Custom NACLs that forget return ports before you master security groups." },
    { title: "RDS and Aurora", body: "The problem before\nYou patch MySQL yourself, miss backups, and put the only copy in one AZ. You open a public database so you can connect from a cafe.\n\nWhat this is\nAmazon RDS is managed MySQL, PostgreSQL, and related engines, with patching, backups, and optional Multi-AZ. Schema and indexes remain your job. Multi-AZ is high availability, not extra reads; replicas are async copies for reads. Aurora is AWS-built MySQL- and PostgreSQL-compatible with shared storage, still in private subnets with no public IP.\n\nWhat it solves\nFailover and backups have a product, not a hope. You still choose size and schema. Reads can scale on replicas if you accept lag.\n\nReal-life example\nA rented garden plot: they water the soil, you choose the plants. Multi-AZ is an understudy waiting in the wings, not a second ticket window for reads. A replica is a photocopier a few seconds behind.\n\nUses\nOLTP for web apps. Interviews: Multi-AZ vs replica, Aurora vs RDS.\n\nWatch out\nPublicly accessible RDS. Pointing the app at a classic Multi-AZ standby for reads. Migrating to Aurora to fix a missing index." },
    { title: "Lambda", body: "The problem before\nYou keep a fleet of VMs running for a few requests an hour. Or you try a twenty-minute batch in a function. Fat packages make the first customer wait.\n\nWhat this is\nAWS Lambda runs code on events without a customer-managed fleet. You pay per request and duration, with a 15-minute maximum, and cold starts happen when a new environment is created. Give each function a least-privilege role; API Gateway, SQS, and EventBridge are common doors. Keep packages small, and use RDS Proxy when many invokes open database connections.\n\nWhat it solves\nSpiky APIs and glue jobs need no idle boxes. Small packages start faster. The proxy reuses hangers so the database is not buried in new connections.\n\nReal-life example\nA fridge light that turns on when the door opens. The first customer of the day may wait while the shop unlocks. A receptionist rings a specialist per visitor when API Gateway sits in front.\n\nUses\nAPIs, queues, EventBridge glue, cron-like rules. Interviews: timeout, cold start, IAM role.\n\nWatch out\nWork longer than 15 minutes. Long-lived keys in the zip. One new RDS connection per invoke. A 100 MB dependency suitcase." },
    { title: "Load balancing and DNS", body: "The problem before\nUsers hit one VM by IP. That box dies and the shop is dark. Sticky sessions pin people to a sick waiter. You treat DNS as a path router.\n\nWhat this is\nAn ALB routes HTTP by path, host, TLS, and optional WAF. An NLB forwards TCP, UDP, or TLS at high speed. Target groups and health checks pick who gets traffic; prefer a shared cache over sticky sessions. Route 53 is DNS, not an HTTP proxy, and alias records can point at an ALB or CloudFront.\n\nWhat it solves\nMany boxes share one name. Unhealthy targets drop out. The phone book can fail over. Web apps usually want the concierge, not the freight tunnel.\n\nReal-life example\nA hotel concierge versus a freight tunnel. Route 53 is the phone book that can also forward if the first number does not answer. Sticky is always getting the same waiter because they remember your order in their head.\n\nUses\nWeb apps on ALB. High-performance TCP on NLB. Names and failover on Route 53. Interviews: ALB vs NLB, alias vs CNAME.\n\nWatch out\nNLB for path-based HTTP. CNAME on the zone apex. Sticky sessions plus an ASG replace. Health checks that query the database so a blink marks every app down." },
    { title: "Observability", body: "The problem before\nCheckout is slow and you have no request id. A leaked key leaves no story. Alarms paint pretty graphs that page nobody.\n\nWhat this is\nAmazon CloudWatch holds metrics, logs, alarms, and dashboards. AWS CloudTrail records who called which AWS API. X-Ray and OpenTelemetry draw traces; correlate logs with request ids. Alarms should page an owner who can act, and lock down the CloudTrail bucket.\n\nWhat it solves\nYou can ask why, not only is it up. Forensics exist after a suspected breach. A line with a nurse attached is an alarm. A line with no nurse is decoration.\n\nReal-life example\nA hospital monitor for the app, CCTV for the control plane, and a subway map of one passenger for a trace. Protect the CCTV tape room.\n\nUses\nEvery production account. Interviews: CloudWatch vs CloudTrail. Latency hunts across Lambda and API Gateway.\n\nWatch out\nCloudTrail off to save money. DEBUG forever with no retention. Tracing every request at 100 percent. Fifty alarms to email that nobody reads." },
    { title: "Pricing", body: "The problem before\nThe bill is a surprise. Idle EC2, unused NAT, and leftover load balancers sit there for months. Nobody owns a tag. NAT carries all S3 traffic.\n\nWhat this is\nOn-demand charges for running capacity. Savings Plans and Reserved Instances discount a usage promise; Spot uses spare capacity and can be interrupted. Internet egress, cross-AZ traffic, and NAT processing are common silent costs. Stop idle EC2, rightsize volumes, lifecycle S3, and tag so the bill has an owner.\n\nWhat it solves\nArchitecture is a cost decision, not only a diagram. You can turn off empty rooms and stop renting unused front doors. Measure before you buy a yearly membership.\n\nReal-life example\nTurning off lights and not renting empty rooms. Receiving postcards is cheap; sending parcels abroad is not. Last-minute airline seats can bump you. A gym membership is cheaper only if you actually go.\n\nUses\nCost reviews. Interviews: egress, NAT, Spot vs on-demand, Savings Plans.\n\nWatch out\nNAT for all S3 traffic when a gateway endpoint would do. Spot for a single irreplaceable database. Buying a three-year size you will not use." },
    { title: "Well-Architected Framework", body: "The problem before\nYou only talk cost, or only security. The design has no named risks. The interview becomes a service dump.\n\nWhat this is\nThe AWS Well-Architected Framework reviews a workload across six pillars: operational excellence, security, reliability, performance efficiency, cost optimization, and sustainability. It is a structured review, not a certificate requirement. Identify one concrete risk per pillar and record leftover gaps.\n\nWhat it solves\nYou present a design as a health checkup with named sections. Interviewers can follow you. Gaps stay honest. The same six-pillar outline is a sound way to talk through a sketch.\n\nReal-life example\nA shop inspection clipboard: kitchen habits, locks, spare generator, speed of service, waste, and lights left on. You do not need a framed certificate to use the clipboard.\n\nUses\nArchitecture interviews. Team reviews even without an official AWS review.\n\nWatch out\nTreating it as a badge hunt. Talking only cost. Listing pillars with no risk attached." },
    { title: "Reference architecture", body: "The problem before\nYou draw random boxes. IAM is missing and everything is one AZ. Keys sit in files, and NAT lives in only one campus.\n\nWhat this is\nA standard web path reaches Route 53, then CloudFront, then an ALB in public subnets. Apps run in private Auto Scaling Groups or ECS, with RDS Multi-AZ, a cache, and S3. Every integration uses an IAM role, not long-lived keys. Say Multi-AZ, NAT or VPC endpoints, CloudWatch, and CloudTrail when you describe the design.\n\nWhat it solves\nYou can sketch a campus map in two minutes. The layers stay in order: name, edge, door, kitchen, vault, coat-check. Security and failover have a spoken place.\n\nReal-life example\nStreet sign, neighborhood brochure rack, front desk, kitchen in the back, register in the vault, coat-check for bags. Staff wear badges, not spare metal keys. Two substations, not one.\n\nUses\nSystem design on AWS. Interview closer: draw the path, then name IAM and AZ.\n\nWatch out\nBoxes with no IAM or AZ story. Public RDS. NAT in only one AZ. latest image tags with no digest." }
  ],
  questions: [
    { id: 1, level: "beginner", q: "What is AWS?", a: "AWS is a cloud: you rent computers, disks, and APIs instead of buying a data center. You pay for what you use. The pieces are services with names like S3 and EC2.\n\nYou still design how the pieces connect. IAM says who may touch which tool. A huge toolbox in many cities that you open with an account.\n\nIn the code: the sketch is users to Route 53, then CloudFront or ALB, then app boxes, then database plus S3. The comment says you rent these APIs; you do not buy the building.\n\nA common mistake is listing twenty service names with no picture of how a request flows.", code: "// architecture sketch (read top to bottom)\n//   users\n//     -> Route 53 (name)\n//     -> CloudFront / ALB\n//     -> app boxes\n//     -> database + S3\n// You rent these APIs; you do not buy the building." },
    { id: 2, level: "beginner", q: "region vs availability zone?", a: "A region is a geography, like ap-south-1 for Mumbai. An availability zone is an isolated place inside that region, like ap-south-1a. Multi-AZ means you can survive one data center failing.\n\nPut databases and load balancers across AZs on purpose. If one substation dies, the others can still run. A city with several separate power substations.\n\nIn the code: the diagram has AZ a with app and db primary, AZ b with app and db standby. Users hit a load balancer in both AZs. One AZ down is not the whole app down.\n\nA common mistake is putting the database in one AZ and calling the app highly available.", code: "// region ap-south-1\n//   AZ a  [ app ] [ db primary ]\n//   AZ b  [ app ] [ db standby ]\n// users -> load balancer in both AZs\n// one AZ down != whole app down" },
    { id: 3, level: "beginner", q: "What is IAM?", a: "IAM answers who can do what on which resource. People and services get identities. Policies are JSON rules.\n\nWithout IAM, a leaked key can do everything. Start here in every AWS design. A building badge that lists which doors open.\n\nIn the code: the policy Version is 2012-10-17. One Statement Allows s3:GetObject on arn:aws:s3:::photos/*. That is read-only for objects in that bucket prefix.\n\nA common mistake is Action * and Resource * on a human user. Then a leak is the whole account.", code: "{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [{\n    \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n    \"Action\": \"s3:GetObject\",\n    \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::photos/*\"\n  }]\n}" },
    { id: 4, level: "beginner", q: "user vs role?", a: "A user is a long-lived identity, often a person. A role is assumed and gives temporary keys. EC2 and Lambda should use roles, not access keys sitting in files.\n\nThe visitor pass is safer to drop. Apps wear roles. An employee badge versus a pass that expires today.\n\nIn the code: the trust policy Allows lambda.amazonaws.com to sts:AssumeRole. That is how a function gets a role instead of a key file.\n\nA common mistake is baking AKIA keys into a Lambda zip. Those keys do not expire with the function.", code: "{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [{\n    \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n    \"Principal\": { \"Service\": \"lambda.amazonaws.com\" },\n    \"Action\": \"sts:AssumeRole\"\n  }]\n}" },
    { id: 5, level: "beginner", q: "What is a policy?", a: "A policy is JSON with Effect, Action, Resource, and optional Condition. Identity policies attach to users or roles. Resource policies sit on things like S3 buckets.\n\nAllow and Deny are the two effects. Deny usually wins when both match. A signed permission slip.\n\nIn the code: Sid ReadPhotos Allows s3:GetObject on arn:aws:s3:::my-photos/*. That is one statement in a larger policy document.\n\nA common mistake is attaching AdministratorAccess to debug, then forgetting to remove it.", code: "{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [{\n    \"Sid\": \"ReadPhotos\",\n    \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n    \"Action\": [\"s3:GetObject\"],\n    \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-photos/*\"\n  }]\n}" },
    { id: 6, level: "beginner", q: "least privilege?", a: "Give only the actions needed on only the resources needed. Start small and add. Avoid AdministratorAccess on humans and on apps.\n\nA leak then has a smaller blast radius. Reviews catch Action * Resource *. A hotel key that opens one room, not every room.\n\nIn the code: Allow s3:PutObject only on arn:aws:s3:::uploads/user-ada/*. Ada can write her prefix, not the whole bucket.\n\nA common mistake is s3:* on * because listing was annoying. Then delete-all is included.", code: "{\n  \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n  \"Action\": [\"s3:PutObject\"],\n  \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::uploads/user-ada/*\"\n}" },
    { id: 7, level: "beginner", q: "access keys vs console login?", a: "Access keys are long-lived API secrets and hurt badly if leaked. Console login is password plus MFA. Prefer SSO and roles.\n\nRotate keys if you must have them. Never commit keys. A metal house key photocopied in email versus a door with a one-time code.\n\nIn the code: the bad idea is commented accessKeyId in source. The good path is a handler that assumes a role with no long-lived key in code.\n\nA common mistake is committing keys 'temporarily'. Git history keeps them.", code: "// bad: keys in source\n// const aws = { accessKeyId: \"AKIA...\", secret: \"...\" };\n// good: the runtime assumes a role; no long-lived key in code\nexports.handler = async () => ({ ok: true });" },
    { id: 8, level: "intermediate", q: "sts assume role?", a: "STS hands out short-lived keys for a role. Used for cross-account access, CI OIDC, and EC2 instance profiles. A session policy can shrink the power even more.\n\nWhen the day ends, the pass dies. That is better than a spare master key. A day pass printed at the desk.\n\nIn the code: another account 999 may AssumeRole if sts:ExternalId equals unique-ticket. ExternalId helps stop a confused deputy.\n\nA common mistake is a trust policy of Principal AWS * with no condition. Anyone could try to assume the role.", code: "{\n  \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n  \"Principal\": { \"AWS\": \"arn:aws:iam::999:root\" },\n  \"Action\": \"sts:AssumeRole\",\n  \"Condition\": {\n    \"StringEquals\": { \"sts:ExternalId\": \"unique-ticket\" }\n  }\n}" },
    { id: 9, level: "intermediate", q: "instance profile?", a: "An instance profile is the glue that attaches a role to a VM. The app asks the metadata service for temporary keys. IMDSv2 is the safer hop-required version.\n\nNo access keys in the image. The nametag is the role. A nametag holder bolted to the machine.\n\nIn the code: the handler returns via instance-profile. The comment says the SDK uses the instance role automatically.\n\nA common mistake is copying ~/.aws/credentials onto the AMI. Every clone then shares one long-lived key.", code: "// app on EC2: no keys in env\nexports.handler = async () => {\n  // SDK uses the instance role automatically\n  return { via: \"instance-profile\" };\n};" },
    { id: 10, level: "advanced", q: "confused deputy / confused role?", a: "A confused deputy is a service tricked into using its power for an attacker. ExternalId and source-account conditions on trust policies help. Do not let random apps fetch instance metadata if they do not need it.\n\nThe receptionist was honest and still caused harm. Tighten the trust policy. A receptionist with master keys who opens a door because a stranger asked nicely.\n\nIn the code: s3.amazonaws.com may AssumeRole only if aws:SourceAccount is 123456789012. That binds the helper to your account.\n\nA common mistake is a trust policy with no SourceAccount or ExternalId on a powerful role.", code: "{\n  \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n  \"Principal\": { \"Service\": \"s3.amazonaws.com\" },\n  \"Action\": \"sts:AssumeRole\",\n  \"Condition\": {\n    \"StringEquals\": { \"aws:SourceAccount\": \"123456789012\" }\n  }\n}" },
    { id: 11, level: "beginner", q: "What is EC2?", a: "EC2 is virtual machines you rent. You pick an image, a size, a disk, and a network. You patch the OS unless a higher service does it for you.\n\nYou pay while it runs. Autoscaling groups manage many of them as cattle. A computer in a locked room in AWS's building.\n\nIn the code: the picture is internet, then a security group, then EC2 running a Node app, then private RDS. Ports 22 and 443 are called out on the group.\n\nA common mistake is a public EC2 with port 22 open to the world. Prefer SSM and private subnets.", code: "// one VM in a picture\n// [ internet ]\n//     |\n// [ security group :22 optional, :443 ]\n//     |\n// [ EC2 : Node app ]\n//     |\n// [ private RDS ]" },
    { id: 12, level: "beginner", q: "AMI?", a: "An AMI is the template for the disk: OS plus maybe your app. A golden AMI is baked in a pipeline so every box starts the same. Launch copies from that photocopy.\n\nDo not hand-install on each VM. Version the AMI like an artifact. A photocopy of a clean laptop disk.\n\nIn the code: launch has ami ami-golden-2026-09 and type t3.micro. console.log shows that recipe.\n\nA common mistake is a unique snowflake AMI per server. Then patching is a scavenger hunt.", code: "// launch template idea\nconst launch = {\n  ami: \"ami-golden-2026-09\",\n  type: \"t3.micro\",\n};\nconsole.log(launch);" },
    { id: 13, level: "beginner", q: "instance types?", a: "Families group hardware: general, compute, memory, burstable, GPU. Size like large scales CPU and RAM. Pick from metrics, not forever guesswork.\n\nA burst family is a small engine with a turbo that can run out. Watch credits on T instances. Choosing a bike, a van, or a truck.\n\nIn the code: types maps t burstable cheap, m balanced, c more CPU, r more RAM. print shows types.m as balanced.\n\nA common mistake is the biggest instance 'to be safe'. Then you pay for idle RAM forever.", code: "const types = {\n  t: \"burstable cheap\",\n  m: \"balanced\",\n  c: \"more CPU\",\n  r: \"more RAM\",\n};\nconsole.log(types.m);" },
    { id: 14, level: "beginner", q: "security group?", a: "A security group is a stateful firewall on a network card. You write allow rules for ports and sources. Inbound default is deny.\n\nPointing at another SG is better than 0.0.0.0/0. Stateful means return traffic is allowed if you allowed the start. A bouncer who remembers the reply packets.\n\nIn the code: ingress from sg-alb on port 443. egress to 0.0.0.0/0 on all ports. The app accepts HTTPS only from the load balancer group.\n\nA common mistake is SSH from 0.0.0.0/0. The whole internet can knock on port 22.", code: "{\n  \"ingress\": [\n    { \"from\": \"sg-alb\", \"port\": 443 }\n  ],\n  \"egress\": [{ \"to\": \"0.0.0.0/0\", \"port\": \"all\" }]\n}" },
    { id: 15, level: "beginner", q: "NACL vs security group?", a: "A NACL is stateless and sits on a subnet, with allow and deny. A security group is stateful and sits on the instance, mostly allows. Most designs live in security groups and leave default NACLs.\n\nStateless means you must allow both directions yourself. Beginners should master SGs first. Neighborhood gate versus apartment door.\n\nIn the code: the path is internet to NACL on the subnet, then security group on the ENI, then the process. The comment says SG remembers the conversation; NACL does not.\n\nA common mistake is a custom NACL that forgets ephemeral return ports. Then nothing works and the SG looks fine.", code: "// packet path\n// internet -> NACL (subnet) -> security group (ENI) -> process\n// SG remembers the conversation; NACL does not\n// teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 16, level: "beginner", q: "key pair vs SSM Session Manager?", a: "A key pair is an SSH key you store and protect. SSM Session Manager uses IAM and does not need port 22 open. Prefer SSM in new designs.\n\nLost SSH keys are painful. IAM can revoke SSM in one policy change. A physical house key versus a timed digital lock tied to your work badge.\n\nIn the code: Allow ssm:StartSession on one instance ARN in ap-south-1. That is a tight shell permission.\n\nA common mistake is sharing one PEM file in Slack. Then you cannot revoke one person.", code: "{\n  \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n  \"Action\": [\"ssm:StartSession\"],\n  \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:ec2:ap-south-1:123:instance/i-abc\"\n}" },
    { id: 17, level: "intermediate", q: "user data?", a: "User data runs when the instance first launches. Good for a little bootstrap. Bad as the only config forever.\n\nPrefer a baked AMI or a real config tool. It is easy to drift. Keep it tiny. A welcome note in a hotel room, not the whole building code.\n\nIn the code: userData is a bash script that echoes ready to /tmp/ok. join with newlines makes cloud-init style text. The log says bootstrap only, not the whole app.\n\nA common mistake is installing the whole app in user data. Then two instances drift on day two.", code: "// user-data idea (cloud-init style text)\nconst userData = [\n  \"#!/bin/bash\",\n  \"echo ready > /tmp/ok\",\n].join(\"\\n\");\nconsole.log(\"bootstrap only, not the whole app\");" },
    { id: 18, level: "intermediate", q: "EBS vs instance store?", a: "EBS is a network disk you can snapshot and move. Instance store is local and dies with the VM. Instance store can be faster and is ephemeral.\n\nPut databases on EBS or RDS. Caches can use instance store if you accept loss. A USB drive you can unplug versus RAM on the motherboard.\n\nIn the code: disks.ebs survives stop and is snapshot-able. instanceStore is gone when the VM dies. print shows the EBS story.\n\nA common mistake is putting a database on instance store. Stop or hardware fail then deletes the data.", code: "// disk story\nconst disks = {\n  ebs: \"survives stop, snapshot-able\",\n  instanceStore: \"gone when the VM dies\",\n};\nconsole.log(disks.ebs);" },
    { id: 19, level: "intermediate", q: "EBS volume types?", a: "gp3 is the usual SSD. io2 is for high IOPS. st1 and sc1 are throughput or cold HDD ideas. Match IOPS to the workload; disks are a common bottleneck.\n\nDo not pay for the race track if you drive to the shop. Watch burst credits on smaller volumes. City road versus race track.\n\nIn the code: volume is gp3, 100 GiB, 3000 iops. console.log shows that choice.\n\nA common mistake is a tiny gp2 volume for a busy database. Then IOPS burst runs out and the app looks 'randomly slow'.", code: "const volume = { type: \"gp3\", sizeGiB: 100, iops: 3000 };\nconsole.log(volume);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments\n// this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 20, level: "intermediate", q: "snapshot?", a: "A snapshot is a point-in-time backup of a volume, stored incrementally. Crash-consistent unless you freeze the filesystem. Copy snapshots to another region for disaster recovery.\n\nTest a restore, not only the snapshot job. If someone was still writing, the photo may be messy. A photo of a whiteboard.\n\nIn the code: snap has volume vol-app, when 2026-09-01, regionCopy ap-south-1 to eu-west-1. print shows that backup idea.\n\nA common mistake is never restoring. The first restore is then during the real failure.", code: "const snap = {\n  volume: \"vol-app\",\n  when: \"2026-09-01\",\n  regionCopy: \"ap-south-1 -> eu-west-1\",\n};\nconsole.log(snap);" },
    { id: 21, level: "intermediate", q: "Auto Scaling Group?", a: "An ASG keeps a count, or a min-max range, of instances from a launch template. It replaces unhealthy ones and can scale on metrics. This is the cattle way to run EC2.\n\nHealth checks must be honest. Tie it to a load balancer. A shop that always has three cashiers, hiring if a line grows.\n\nIn the code: asg has min 2, max 8, desired 3, ami ami-golden. The comment says unhealthy instance gets replaced, not pet-fixed.\n\nA common mistake is desired 1 with no load balancer. Then one bad box is the whole site.", code: "const asg = { min: 2, max: 8, desired: 3, ami: \"ami-golden\" };\nconsole.log(asg);\n// unhealthy instance -> replace, not pet-fix\n// teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 22, level: "intermediate", q: "launch template?", a: "A launch template stores AMI, size, security group, user data, and IAM role. ASGs use it. Prefer it over old launch configurations.\n\nChange the cutter, roll a new batch. Pin versions in the ASG. A cookie cutter with a version number.\n\nIn the code: template version 5 has ami-123, sg-web, role-web. print shows that recipe.\n\nA common mistake is editing instances by hand after launch. The next scale-out boots the old template.", code: "const template = {\n  version: 5,\n  ami: \"ami-123\",\n  sg: \"sg-web\",\n  role: \"role-web\",\n};\nconsole.log(template);" },
    { id: 23, level: "beginner", q: "What is S3?", a: "S3 stores objects at keys inside buckets. It is huge, durable, and reached over HTTP APIs. It is not a normal Linux filesystem.\n\nYou do not cd into S3 like a folder, even if keys have slashes. Durability is the famous 11 nines story. A coat-check: bag in, ticket out.\n\nIn the code: object has bucket my-photos, key 2026/cat.jpg, body bytes. print shows the key.\n\nA common mistake is treating S3 like a shared disk for a database. It is object storage, not POSIX.", code: "const object = {\n  bucket: \"my-photos\",\n  key: \"2026/cat.jpg\",\n  body: \"<bytes>\",\n};\nconsole.log(object.key);" },
    { id: 24, level: "beginner", q: "bucket naming and global namespace?", a: "Bucket names are globally unique across AWS. Pick a prefix with your account or org. The region is chosen when you create the bucket.\n\nmy-photos may already be taken. Plan names in IaC. A unique email address, not a folder name on one laptop.\n\nIn the code: bucket name is acme-prod-photos-apsouth1 in region ap-south-1. print shows that unique name.\n\nA common mistake is a short cute name that collides. Create then fails in CI.", code: "const bucket = {\n  name: \"acme-prod-photos-apsouth1\",\n  region: \"ap-south-1\",\n};\nconsole.log(bucket.name);" },
    { id: 25, level: "beginner", q: "S3 durability vs availability?", a: "Durability is we will not lose the object. Availability is you can download it right now. People mix them up.\n\nStandard class aims at very high durability. A regional outage can still block reads. A book in a vault versus the library being open today.\n\nIn the code: s3 maps durability to almost never lose the bytes and availability to can I GET it this minute. print shows both promises.\n\nA common mistake is quoting 11 nines as uptime. That number is about not losing bytes.", code: "const s3 = {\n  durability: \"almost never lose the bytes\",\n  availability: \"can I GET it this minute?\",\n};\nconsole.log(s3);" },
    { id: 26, level: "intermediate", q: "storage classes?", a: "Hot data stays in Standard. Cold data moves to cheaper slower classes. Lifecycle rules move or expire objects.\n\nIntelligent-Tiering can shuffle for you. Retrieval time is the price of cheap storage. Do not Glacier something you need in 50 ms. Desk, closet, warehouse.\n\nIn the code: lifecycle afterDays 30 moveTo STANDARD_IA. print shows that rule.\n\nA common mistake is Glacier for thumbnails the website needs on every page load. Then every GET waits.", code: "const lifecycle = {\n  afterDays: 30,\n  moveTo: \"STANDARD_IA\",\n};\nconsole.log(lifecycle);" },
    { id: 27, level: "intermediate", q: "S3 versioning?", a: "Versioning stores prior versions when you overwrite or delete. You can recover from an oops. MFA delete and Object Lock help against ransomware-style deletes.\n\nIt costs more storage. Turn it on for important buckets. Google Docs version history for files.\n\nIn the code: contract.pdf has versions v1-bytes and v2-bytes. The log says restore means pick an old version id.\n\nA common mistake is versioning off, then a bad deploy overwrites the only copy.", code: "const object = {\n  key: \"contract.pdf\",\n  versions: [\"v1-bytes\", \"v2-bytes\"],\n};\nconsole.log(\"restore means pick an old version id\");" },
    { id: 28, level: "intermediate", q: "presigned URL?", a: "Your API checks the user, then mints a temporary signed URL. The browser PUTs or GETs that URL. Expiry must be short.\n\nThe valet ticket is not your house key. Never mint a week-long PUT to a public-looking path without thought. A valet ticket that works for 10 minutes.\n\nIn the code: the handler builds key from event.user plus photo.jpg and returns a fake signed uploadUrl. The comment says the SDK would create a signed PUT.\n\nA common mistake is a public bucket so the browser can upload. Then anyone can write.", code: "exports.handler = async (event) => {\n  const key = event.user + \"/photo.jpg\";\n  // SDK would create a signed PUT URL for key\n  return { uploadUrl: \"https://s3.example/signed...\", key };\n};" },
    { id: 29, level: "intermediate", q: "Block Public Access?", a: "Block Public Access stops accidental public ACLs and policies. Turn it on unless you truly host a public website. Public buckets are a classic leak.\n\nAccount-level block catches new buckets too. Prefer CloudFront for public files. A master deadbolt that ignores please leave the door open notes.\n\nIn the code: a Deny for Principal * s3:GetObject when aws:SecureTransport is false. That extra statement forces HTTPS on a private bucket.\n\nA common mistake is turning Block Public Access off to 'make the website work'. Then the bucket is a leak waiting.", code: "{\n  \"Statement\": [{\n    \"Effect\": \"Deny\",\n    \"Principal\": \"*\",\n    \"Action\": \"s3:GetObject\",\n    \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::private-bucket/*\",\n    \"Condition\": { \"Bool\": { \"aws:SecureTransport\": \"false\" } }\n  }]\n}" },
    { id: 30, level: "intermediate", q: "S3 encryption?", a: "SSE-S3, SSE-KMS, SSE-C, or you encrypt before upload. Encryption at rest is table stakes. KMS adds an audit of who decrypted.\n\nHTTPS protects data on the wire too. Bucket defaults can force encryption. A locked filing cabinet versus a cabinet whose key-use is logged.\n\nIn the code: Deny PutObject on secure/* unless s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption is aws:kms. Unencrypted puts are blocked.\n\nA common mistake is encryption at rest with a key policy that allows everyone. Then encryption is a checkbox.", code: "{\n  \"Effect\": \"Deny\",\n  \"Action\": \"s3:PutObject\",\n  \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::secure/*\",\n  \"Condition\": {\n    \"StringNotEquals\": { \"s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption\": \"aws:kms\" }\n  }\n}" },
    { id: 31, level: "intermediate", q: "S3 consistency?", a: "Since 2020, S3 has strong read-after-write consistency for new puts, overwrites, and deletes. Old interview answers about eventual for overwrite are outdated for current S3.\n\nYou can still have app-level caches that lie. Know the date in interviews. A mailbox that now shows the new letter immediately.\n\nIn the code: after PutObject of cat.jpg, GetObject sees v2. story maps put v2 get v2.\n\nA common mistake is reciting 2013 eventual-consistency lore as if it were current S3.", code: "// after PutObject of key cat.jpg\n// GetObject of cat.jpg sees that version (current S3)\nconst story = { put: \"v2\", get: \"v2\" };\nconsole.log(story);" },
    { id: 32, level: "intermediate", q: "CloudFront?", a: "CloudFront caches at edge locations close to users. It can sit in front of S3 or an ALB. TLS and WAF plug in here.\n\nInvalidate carefully; versioned file names are easier. Private buckets can stay private with OAC. Neighborhood mini-libraries instead of one far warehouse.\n\nIn the code: path is user -> CloudFront -> S3. The comments say cache hit means no origin trip.\n\nA common mistake is invalidating /* on every deploy. That is slow and expensive; hash the filenames instead.", code: "// user -> edge cache -> origin (S3 or ALB)\n// cache hit: no origin trip\nconst path = \"user -> CloudFront -> S3\";\nconsole.log(path);" },
    { id: 33, level: "beginner", q: "What is a VPC?", a: "A VPC is your private network in AWS with an IP range, subnets, and routes. Most resources launch into a VPC. The fence is not security by itself; IAM and SGs still matter.\n\nPlan IP ranges so you can peer later. Default VPC is for learning, not a careful prod design. A fenced campus with internal roads.\n\nIn the code: VPC 10.0.0.0/16 has public 10.0.1.0/24 plus IGW, private 10.0.2.0/24 plus NAT, data 10.0.3.0/24.\n\nA common mistake is one huge public subnet for app and database. Then the database has a path to the internet.", code: "// VPC 10.0.0.0/16\n//   public subnet  10.0.1.0/24  + IGW\n//   private subnet 10.0.2.0/24  + NAT\n//   data subnet    10.0.3.0/24" },
    { id: 34, level: "beginner", q: "public vs private subnet?", a: "A public subnet has a route to an Internet Gateway and often public IPs. A private subnet has no direct IGW path; outbound uses NAT. Databases belong in private subnets.\n\nApp boxes often sit private too, behind a load balancer. Public does not mean no security group. Shop window on the street versus a safe in the back room.\n\nIn the code: public ALB, private app ASG, private RDS. Users go user to ALB to app to RDS. RDS has no IGW route.\n\nA common mistake is a public RDS 'so I can connect from home'. Use a bastion or SSM port forward instead.", code: "// public:  ALB\n// private: app ASG\n// private: RDS\n// arrows: user->ALB->app->RDS\n// RDS has no IGW route" },
    { id: 35, level: "intermediate", q: "IGW vs NAT gateway?", a: "An Internet Gateway lets resources with public IPs talk both ways. A NAT Gateway lets private instances start outbound only. NAT is AZ-bound and costs hourly plus data.\n\nPut NAT in more than one AZ for HA or use endpoints. NAT bills surprise people. Front door versus a one-way mail slot out.\n\nIn the code: comments show private app to NAT to internet outbound, internet to IGW to ALB inbound. Internet cannot open a connection straight to a private IP.\n\nA common mistake is one NAT in AZ a only. Private apps in AZ b then die when AZ a dies.", code: "// private app -> NAT -> internet (outbound)\n// internet -> IGW -> ALB (inbound)\n// internet cannot open a connection straight to private IP\n// teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 36, level: "intermediate", q: "route table?", a: "A route table lists destinations: local, IGW, NAT, peering, transit gateway. The most common no internet bug is a missing route. Each subnet is associated with a table.\n\nRead the table when debugging. Wrong sign, wrong city. Road signs at every intersection.\n\nIn the code: routes are 10.0.0.0/16 local and 0.0.0.0/0 igw-xxx. print shows a public subnet table.\n\nA common mistake is associating a private subnet with the public table. Then instances get public routes you did not intend.", code: "const routes = [\n  { dest: \"10.0.0.0/16\", target: \"local\" },\n  { dest: \"0.0.0.0/0\", target: \"igw-xxx\" },\n];\nconsole.log(routes);" },
    { id: 37, level: "intermediate", q: "VPC peering vs Transit Gateway?", a: "Peering is one-to-one and not transitive. Transit Gateway is a hub for many VPCs. Peering is simpler for two VPCs.\n\nNon-transitive means A-B and B-C does not give A-C. Plan IP overlap before you peer. A private dirt path versus a roundabout for a neighborhood.\n\nIn the code: peering is VPC A to VPC B only. TGW is A, B, and C all on a hub.\n\nA common mistake is overlapping CIDRs. Then peering cannot route and you rebuild VPCs.", code: "// peering: VPC A <--> VPC B   (only those two)\n// TGW:     A \\\n//          B -- hub\n//          C /" },
    { id: 38, level: "intermediate", q: "What are AWS PrivateLink and VPC endpoints?", a: "Definition. A VPC endpoint keeps traffic between a VPC and an AWS service on the Amazon network. Gateway endpoints exist for Amazon S3 and Amazon DynamoDB and are specified as prefix-list routes in a route table. Interface endpoints, provided by AWS PrivateLink, place an elastic network interface in a subnet and resolve the service to a private IP. IAM still authorizes every API call; the endpoint only changes the network path.\n\nHow it works. Private subnets can reach supported APIs without a public IP, an Internet Gateway, or a NAT Gateway. Gateway endpoints do not use a security group. Interface endpoints do, and they incur hourly and data-processing charges. Both types accept an endpoint policy that further restricts principals, actions, and resources. DNS entries for the service can be overridden so the official hostname resolves inside the VPC.\n\nConfiguration. Bind object access to a specific endpoint with aws:SourceVpce. The statement below allows s3 actions on arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket/* only when the request enters through vpce-123.\n\nOperational risk. Sending S3 or DynamoDB traffic from private subnets through a NAT Gateway incurs per-gigabyte NAT charges and exposes an unnecessary public path. Prefer a gateway endpoint, then tighten IAM and the endpoint policy. Deploy interface endpoints in more than one Availability Zone if the workload must survive an AZ failure.", code: "{\n  \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n  \"Action\": \"s3:*\",\n  \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket/*\",\n  \"Condition\": {\n    \"StringEquals\": { \"aws:SourceVpce\": \"vpce-123\" }\n  }\n}" },
    { id: 39, level: "advanced", q: "CIDR planning?", a: "Do not reuse the same 10.0.0.0/16 in every VPC if you will peer. Leave room to grow. A /16 VPC with /20 subnets is a common start.\n\nOverlap is a painful later surprise. Draw it before you click. Phone numbers that must stay unique when two companies merge.\n\nIn the code: plan vpc 10.20.0.0/16, public 10.20.0.0/20, private 10.20.16.0/20. print shows that layout.\n\nA common mistake is copy-paste 10.0.0.0/16 for every account. Then you cannot peer or use Transit Gateway cleanly.", code: "const plan = {\n  vpc: \"10.20.0.0/16\",\n  public: \"10.20.0.0/20\",\n  private: \"10.20.16.0/20\",\n};\nconsole.log(plan);" },
    { id: 40, level: "intermediate", q: "Elastic IP?", a: "An Elastic IP can remap to another instance. Idle EIPs cost money now. Prefer DNS plus a load balancer over pinning IPs on app servers.\n\nIPv4 is scarce. Do not collect unused EIPs. A famous phone number that can point at a new desk.\n\nIn the code: eip has a publicIp and attachedTo nat-or-bastion. The log says prefer ALB DNS for apps.\n\nA common mistake is an EIP on every app server. Then you cannot autoscale without DNS pain.", code: "const eip = { publicIp: \"x.x.x.x\", attachedTo: \"nat-or-bastion\" };\nconsole.log(\"prefer ALB DNS for apps\");\n// teaching snippet: read the comments\n// this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 41, level: "beginner", q: "What is RDS?", a: "RDS is managed MySQL, Postgres, and friends. AWS helps with patching, backups, and Multi-AZ. You still choose size, schema, and indexes.\n\nYou still pay for idle instances. Put it in private subnets. A rented garden plot: they water the soil, you choose the plants.\n\nIn the code: rds engine postgres, multiAZ true. The sketch is app private to RDS private with no public IP.\n\nA common mistake is PubliclyAccessible true so you can connect from a cafe. Use a bastion or SSM.", code: "// app (private) --> RDS (private, Multi-AZ)\n// no public IP on the database\nconst rds = { engine: \"postgres\", multiAZ: true };\nconsole.log(rds);" },
    { id: 42, level: "intermediate", q: "RDS Multi-AZ?", a: "Multi-AZ keeps a synchronous standby in another AZ. Failover is automatic. The classic standby is not a read replica; you do not send reads there.\n\nYou pay extra for the spare. This is HA, not extra read scale. An understudy actor who knows every line, waiting in the wings.\n\nIn the code: comments show AZ a primary writes, AZ b standby sync copy not for reads. Failover points the endpoint at the new primary.\n\nA common mistake is pointing the app at the standby for reads. Classic Multi-AZ standby is not for that.", code: "// AZ a: primary  (writes)\n// AZ b: standby  (sync copy, not for reads)\n// failover: DNS/endpoint points at the new primary\n// teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 43, level: "intermediate", q: "read replica?", a: "A replica is an async copy. There can be lag. You can promote it if the primary dies, manual or Aurora-style.\n\nGood for reporting queries. Writes still go to the primary. A photocopier that is a few seconds behind.\n\nIn the code: db write primary, read replica, lagMs 120. print shows that split.\n\nA common mistake is a read replica and assuming zero lag. A user may not see their own write yet.", code: "// primary --async--> replica (reads)\nconst db = { write: \"primary\", read: \"replica\", lagMs: 120 };\nconsole.log(db);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 44, level: "intermediate", q: "Aurora?", a: "Aurora is AWS-built MySQL/Postgres compatible with shared storage. Failover can be faster. Replicas are easier.\n\nPricing and limits differ. It is still a relational database. You still design schema. Not magic for a bad query. A custom engine in a familiar car body.\n\nIn the code: aurora writer 1, readers 2. Comments say storage is shared and compute nodes attach.\n\nA common mistake is migrating to Aurora to fix a missing index. The query is still bad.", code: "// Aurora storage is shared; compute nodes attach\n// writer + readers\nconst aurora = { writer: 1, readers: 2 };\nconsole.log(aurora);" },
    { id: 45, level: "intermediate", q: "RDS backup / PITR?", a: "Automated backups plus logs allow point-in-time restore inside the window. Snapshots are extra manual photos. Test restores.\n\nRetention is a product choice. If you never slide the timeline, you do not know it works. A camera plus a timeline slider.\n\nIn the code: backup windowDays 7, pitr restore to 15:03 yesterday. print shows that rewind idea.\n\nA common mistake is retention of 1 day and a bug found on day 3. The good copy is gone.", code: "const backup = {\n  windowDays: 7,\n  pitr: \"restore to 15:03 yesterday\",\n};\nconsole.log(backup);" },
    { id: 46, level: "beginner", q: "DynamoDB?", a: "DynamoDB is a managed NoSQL key-value store. It is fast at huge scale if you design keys for your access patterns first. It is not RDS.\n\nYou ask by key, not by any random column, unless you add indexes. On-demand vs provisioned is a billing choice. A giant labeled locker wall, not a SQL spreadsheet.\n\nIn the code: item pk USER#ada, sk PROFILE, name Ada. The comment says query by pk, not WHERE city =.\n\nA common mistake is treating Dynamo like Postgres with any-column filters. You will scan the table and pay.", code: "const item = { pk: \"USER#ada\", sk: \"PROFILE\", name: \"Ada\" };\nconsole.log(item);\n// query by pk (and maybe sk), not \"WHERE city =\"\n// teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 47, level: "intermediate", q: "Dynamo partition key?", a: "The partition key chooses the partition. Hot keys throttle. A sort key lets you query a range inside the partition.\n\nSpread writes across many partition keys. One celebrity user as the only key is a trap. Filing cabinet drawer and folders in order.\n\nIn the code: two items share pk ORDER#1001 with sk ITEM#1 and ITEM#2. You can query that order's items as a range.\n\nA common mistake is pk = \"ALL\" for every item. Then one partition takes all the heat.", code: "const keys = [\n  { pk: \"ORDER#1001\", sk: \"ITEM#1\" },\n  { pk: \"ORDER#1001\", sk: \"ITEM#2\" },\n];\nconsole.log(keys);" },
    { id: 48, level: "intermediate", q: "Dynamo GSI/LSI?", a: "Secondary indexes let you query other patterns. A GSI has its own keys and its own capacity. It can be eventually consistent and it costs money.\n\nDesign indexes with the same care as the table. You pay for the extra catalog. A second card catalog in a library.\n\nIn the code: GSI pk email, sk userId. The comment says query by email via GSI while the table PK is userId.\n\nA common mistake is a GSI on a high-cardinality changing field with no capacity plan. Then the index is the outage.", code: "// table PK userId  -- GSI PK email\nconst gsi = { pk: \"email\", sk: \"userId\" };\nconsole.log(\"query by email via GSI\", gsi);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 49, level: "beginner", q: "ElastiCache?", a: "ElastiCache is a fast cache: sessions, rate limits, hot keys. It is not your system of record unless you design persistence carefully. If Redis dies, the database must still know the truth.\n\nPut it in private subnets. Watch eviction. A notepad on the fridge, not the land registry.\n\nIn the code: cache key session:ada, ttlSeconds 3600. The comment says miss, load from RDS, then set cache.\n\nA common mistake is storing the only copy of an order in Redis. A flush then loses money.", code: "const cache = { key: \"session:ada\", ttlSeconds: 3600 };\nconsole.log(cache);\n// miss -> load from RDS, then set cache\n// teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 50, level: "beginner", q: "What is Lambda?", a: "Lambda runs your code on events without you managing VMs. You pay per request and duration. Max time is 15 minutes. Disk is limited. Cold starts exist.\n\nGreat for glue and spiky APIs. Give each function a tight IAM role. A light that turns on when someone opens the fridge.\n\nIn the code: handler reads event.queryStringParameters.name or world. It returns statusCode 200 and a JSON hello body.\n\nA common mistake is a 20-minute batch in Lambda. The hard limit is 15 minutes.", code: "exports.handler = async (event) => {\n  const name = event.queryStringParameters?.name || \"world\";\n  return {\n    statusCode: 200,\n    body: JSON.stringify({ hello: name }),\n  };\n};" },
    { id: 51, level: "intermediate", q: "cold start?", a: "A new execution environment must load your runtime and code. VPC, fat packages, and Java make it heavier. Provisioned concurrency keeps environments warm for a price.\n\nLater customers walk into a lit shop. Keep packages small. A shop that must unlock and turn on lights for the first customer.\n\nIn the code: the handler records Date.now at start and returns ms elapsed. The comment says first call in a new env inits the SDK, then work.\n\nA common mistake is a 100 MB dependency zip 'just in case'. Cold starts then dominate latency.", code: "exports.handler = async () => {\n  // first call in a new env: init SDK, then work\n  const started = Date.now();\n  return { ms: Date.now() - started };\n};" },
    { id: 52, level: "intermediate", q: "Lambda + API Gateway?", a: "API Gateway takes HTTPS, then invokes Lambda. You get TLS, throttling, and auth options without an always-on server. Watch timeouts: gateway and function must both be long enough.\n\nHTTP APIs are a modern flavor. Return statusCode and body as JSON strings in the handler. A receptionist who rings a specialist per visitor.\n\nIn the code: method comes from event.requestContext.http.method. GET returns 200 with {\"ok\":true}. Anything else returns 405.\n\nA common mistake is returning a raw object with no statusCode. API Gateway then does not know the HTTP code.", code: "exports.handler = async (event) => {\n  const method = event.requestContext?.http?.method;\n  if (method === \"GET\") {\n    return { statusCode: 200, body: \"{\\\"ok\\\":true}\" };\n  }\n  return { statusCode: 405, body: \"{\\\"error\\\":\\\"method\\\"}\" };\n};" },
    { id: 53, level: "intermediate", q: "Lambda in a VPC?", a: "You put Lambda in a VPC to reach private RDS. That adds networking parts. RDS Proxy helps so you do not open a new DB connection per invoke.\n\nThe badge setup has a cost. Prefer the proxy for connection storms. Needing a campus badge to enter the database building.\n\nIn the code: the handler returns db via-proxy. The comment says inside VPC, connect to RDS via the proxy hostname.\n\nA common mistake is one new RDS connection per Lambda invoke at scale. The database max_connections then dies.", code: "exports.handler = async () => {\n  // inside VPC: connect to RDS via RDS Proxy hostname\n  return { db: \"via-proxy\" };\n};" },
    { id: 54, level: "intermediate", q: "RDS Proxy?", a: "Lambda can create too many connections. The proxy multiplexes them. Still use private networking. Still use IAM or secrets for auth.\n\nServerless and relational DBs need this pattern often. A coat-check that reuses hangers instead of buying one per guest.\n\nIn the code: cfg host is app.proxy.region.rds.amazonaws.com with max 100. The log says app talks to proxy, proxy talks to RDS.\n\nA common mistake is pointing Lambda at the RDS instance hostname directly under burst traffic. Connections explode.", code: "const cfg = { host: \"app.proxy.region.rds.amazonaws.com\", max: 100 };\nconsole.log(\"app talks to proxy, proxy talks to RDS\");\n// teaching snippet: read the comments\n// this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 55, level: "beginner", q: "SQS?", a: "Producers send messages. Consumers poll and delete. It decouples services and smooths spikes. Delivery is at-least-once, so handlers must be idempotent.\n\nUse a DLQ for poison messages. If the clerk processes twice, charging twice is bad. A ticket queue at a shop.\n\nIn the code: the handler loops event.Records. onceKey is rec.messageId. The comment says process using onceKey so retries are safe.\n\nA common mistake is charging a card on every receive with no dedupe key. SQS retries then double-charge.", code: "exports.handler = async (event) => {\n  for (const rec of event.Records || []) {\n    const onceKey = rec.messageId;\n    // process using onceKey so retries are safe\n    console.log(\"msg\", onceKey);\n  }\n};" },
    { id: 56, level: "intermediate", q: "SQS standard vs FIFO?", a: "Standard: huge throughput, best-effort order, possible duplicates. FIFO: order per group, extra exactly-once help, lower throughput. Pick FIFO only if order is a real requirement.\n\nIdempotency still matters. A busy cafeteria line versus numbered tickets at a tiny counter.\n\nIn the code: queues.standard has best effort order and possible dups. fifo has order per group and suffix .fifo. print shows the fifo object.\n\nA common mistake is FIFO for everything. You pay with throughput limits you did not need.", code: "const queues = {\n  standard: { order: \"best effort\", dups: \"possible\" },\n  fifo: { order: \"per group\", suffix: \".fifo\" },\n};\nconsole.log(queues.fifo);" },
    { id: 57, level: "intermediate", q: "visibility timeout?", a: "After a consumer gets a message, it is hidden for N seconds. If you do not delete it in time, it reappears. Too short: double work. Too long: slow retries.\n\nMatch timeout to worst-case processing. Extend if work is long. A library book on your desk until you return it or time runs out.\n\nIn the code: vis seconds 60, workMs 5000. The log says hide while working, then delete.\n\nA common mistake is visibility 30 seconds and work that takes 2 minutes. Then two workers process the same message.", code: "const vis = { seconds: 60, workMs: 5000 };\nconsole.log(\"hide while working, then delete\");\n// teaching snippet: read the comments\n// this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 58, level: "intermediate", q: "DLQ?", a: "Messages that fail N times go to a DLQ. Alarm on depth. Do not let it fill in silence.\n\nSomeone must look in the bin. Fix the consumer or the payload. A broken-item bin in a factory.\n\nIn the code: flow main orders, afterFails 5, dlq orders-dlq. print shows that path.\n\nA common mistake is a DLQ with no alarm. Poison messages pile up for months.", code: "const flow = { main: \"orders\", afterFails: 5, dlq: \"orders-dlq\" };\nconsole.log(flow);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments\n// this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 59, level: "beginner", q: "SNS?", a: "SNS is pub/sub: one message, many subscribers. Subscribers can be SQS, Lambda, email, HTTP. Fan-out is the word.\n\nSNS to SQS is a durable worker pattern. Each classroom has its own queue if it must not lose the message. A school loudspeaker: one announcement, many classrooms.\n\nIn the code: fanout is sqs-email and lambda-stock. Comments show topic orders to both workers.\n\nA common mistake is HTTP subscribe only with no retry queue. A down subscriber then loses events.", code: "// topic \"orders\" -> SQS email-worker\n//                 -> Lambda inventory\nconst fanout = [\"sqs-email\", \"lambda-stock\"];\nconsole.log(fanout);" },
    { id: 60, level: "intermediate", q: "EventBridge?", a: "EventBridge routes events with rules, archive, and replay. Sources can be AWS, your apps, or SaaS. Keep payloads small.\n\nUse a schema if the team is large. It replaces many old CloudWatch Events stories. A train station timetable: when event X, send car Y.\n\nIn the code: rule source app.orders, detailType OrderPaid, target lambda-fulfill. print shows that route.\n\nA common mistake is a giant payload in the event. Then you hit size limits and cannot replay cleanly.", code: "const rule = {\n  source: \"app.orders\",\n  detailType: \"OrderPaid\",\n  target: \"lambda-fulfill\",\n};\nconsole.log(rule);" },
    { id: 61, level: "beginner", q: "ALB vs NLB?", a: "ALB understands HTTP, paths, hosts, WAF, OIDC. NLB is TCP/UDP/TLS, static IPs, very fast. Pick ALB for web apps.\n\nHealth checks still matter on both. TLS often ends at the ALB. Hotel concierge versus a freight tunnel.\n\nIn the code: alb layer 7, routes /api and /. Comments show user HTTPS to ALB path /api to target group to app:3000.\n\nA common mistake is NLB for a path-based HTTP app. Then you lose host and path routing.", code: "// user HTTPS -> ALB (path /api) -> target group -> app:3000\nconst alb = { layer: 7, routes: [\"/api\", \"/\"] };\nconsole.log(alb);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 62, level: "intermediate", q: "target group health checks?", a: "The load balancer only sends traffic to healthy targets. If /health hits the database, a DB blink marks every app down. Use a cheap check.\n\nReturn 200 quickly. Separate deep checks for ops dashboards. A temperature check at the door, not a full surgery.\n\nIn the code: handler returns statusCode 200 body ok. Comments split liveness as process up versus readiness as ready for users, maybe skip DB.\n\nA common mistake is health = SELECT 1 on the primary. A brief replica lag then takes the site down.", code: "exports.handler = async () => ({ statusCode: 200, body: \"ok\" });\n// liveness: process up\n// readiness: ready for users (maybe skip DB)\n// teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 63, level: "intermediate", q: "sticky sessions?", a: "Stickiness helps legacy in-memory sessions. Prefer stateless apps plus Redis or JWT. If that waiter is sick, your session is sick.\n\nSticky is a crutch. Design it out when you can. Always getting the same waiter because they remember your order in their head.\n\nIn the code: session store redis, sticky false. print shows the preferred design.\n\nA common mistake is sticky sessions plus an ASG replace. Users then lose carts when their instance dies.", code: "const session = { store: \"redis\", sticky: false };\nconsole.log(session);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments\n// this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 64, level: "beginner", q: "Route 53?", a: "Route 53 stores records, health checks, and routing policies. Alias records can point at ALB, CloudFront, or S3 websites. TTL is how long old answers stick in caches.\n\nHealth checks drive failover policies. It is DNS, not a web server. A phone book that can also fail over.\n\nIn the code: rec name www.example.com alias alb-xxx.amazonaws.com. print shows that record.\n\nA common mistake is treating Route 53 as a load balancer for HTTP paths. It answers names, not /api routes.", code: "const rec = { name: \"www.example.com\", alias: \"alb-xxx.amazonaws.com\" };\nconsole.log(rec);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments\n// this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 65, level: "intermediate", q: "alias vs CNAME?", a: "Alias is AWS-specific and can sit on the zone apex pointing at ALB or CloudFront. A normal CNAME cannot sit at the apex on standard DNS. Apex is the bare example.com.\n\nUse alias there. CNAME is fine for www in many setups. A special shortcut the phone company allows only for their own buildings.\n\nIn the code: dns apex alias, www cname-or-alias. Comments show example.com Alias A to ALB.\n\nA common mistake is a CNAME on the apex. Many DNS hosts reject it or break MX mail.", code: "// apex: example.com  -> Alias A to ALB\n// www:  CNAME or Alias to the same target\nconst dns = { apex: \"alias\", www: \"cname-or-alias\" };\nconsole.log(dns);" },
    { id: 66, level: "beginner", q: "CloudWatch?", a: "CloudWatch holds metrics, logs, alarms, and dashboards. Logs Insights queries log lines. Alarms should page a human who can act.\n\nPut request ids in logs. A line with no nurse is decoration. A hospital monitor.\n\nIn the code: a JSON log has level error, msg pay failed, request_id abc. That line can be queried later.\n\nA common mistake is DEBUG logs with no retention plan. Then you pay for noise and cannot find the error.", code: "console.log(JSON.stringify({\n  level: \"error\",\n  msg: \"pay failed\",\n  request_id: \"abc\",\n}));" },
    { id: 67, level: "intermediate", q: "CloudTrail?", a: "CloudTrail records who called which AWS API. Enable in all regions. Store in S3. It is the first story after a suspected breach.\n\nProtect the trail bucket. No trail, no forensics. CCTV for the control plane, not for your app's HTTP.\n\nIn the code: eventName DeleteBucket, userIdentity arn for user ada, sourceIPAddress 203.0.113.5. That is one audit event.\n\nA common mistake is CloudTrail off to save money. Then a leaked key leaves no story.", code: "{\n  \"eventName\": \"DeleteBucket\",\n  \"userIdentity\": { \"arn\": \"arn:aws:iam::123:user/ada\" },\n  \"sourceIPAddress\": \"203.0.113.5\"\n}" },
    { id: 68, level: "intermediate", q: "X-Ray / ADOT?", a: "X-Ray draws traces. OpenTelemetry (ADOT) is the direction of travel. Useful for Lambda plus API Gateway latency hunts.\n\nPass the trace header. Sample so you do not drown. A subway map of one passenger's trip. You see which stop ate 800 ms.\n\nIn the code: handler reads x-amzn-trace-id from event.headers and logs it. Returns 200 with {}.\n\nA common mistake is tracing every request at 100% in prod. Then the tracer is the outage.", code: "exports.handler = async (event) => {\n  const trace = event.headers?.[\"x-amzn-trace-id\"];\n  console.log(\"trace\", trace);\n  return { statusCode: 200, body: \"{}\" };\n};" },
    { id: 69, level: "beginner", q: "What is ECS?", a: "ECS runs Docker tasks on EC2 or Fargate. It is simpler than EKS if you do not need Kubernetes APIs. Task definition is the recipe.\n\nService keeps N tasks. IAM still wraps every task. A parking garage that knows how to start container cars.\n\nIn the code: task image app:sha, cpu 256, memory 512. print shows that recipe.\n\nA common mistake is latest tags in the task definition. Then you cannot say which git commit is running.", code: "const task = { image: \"app:sha\", cpu: 256, memory: 512 };\nconsole.log(task);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments\n// this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 70, level: "beginner", q: "Fargate?", a: "Fargate runs the task without you managing EC2. You pay for vCPU and memory of the task. There is some provisioning delay.\n\nGreat default for many teams. Still need VPC, logs, and IAM. A taxi instead of owning the car.\n\nIn the code: fargate launchType FARGATE, size 0.25 vCPU. print shows that choice.\n\nA common mistake is Fargate with public IP on every task for no reason. Prefer private subnets plus a load balancer.", code: "const fargate = { launchType: \"FARGATE\", size: \"0.25 vCPU\" };\nconsole.log(fargate);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments\n// this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 71, level: "beginner", q: "EKS?", a: "EKS is a managed Kubernetes control plane. You still manage nodes or Fargate profiles, add-ons, and YAML. Use it when you want the k8s ecosystem.\n\nIt is not no ops. Skills transfer across clouds. AWS running the airport tower; you still fly the planes.\n\nIn the code: eks controlPlane managed, nodes your node group. Comments say same k8s ideas: Deployment, Service.\n\nA common mistake is picking EKS for fashion when ECS would ship the same containers with less YAML.", code: "// same k8s ideas: Deployment, Service\n// EKS hosts the API server for you\nconst eks = { controlPlane: \"managed\", nodes: \"your node group\" };\nconsole.log(eks);" },
    { id: 72, level: "intermediate", q: "ECS vs EKS vs Elastic Beanstalk vs App Runner?", a: "Beanstalk and App Runner are more PaaS. ECS is AWS-native containers. EKS is Kubernetes. Interviews want why: team skills and lock-in.\n\nStart simple if the team is small. Do not pick EKS for fashion. Bus, train, or airplane for the same trip.\n\nIn the code: pick paas App Runner / Beanstalk, containers ECS, k8s EKS. print shows that menu.\n\nA common mistake is EKS on day one with two engineers. The cluster becomes the product.", code: "const pick = {\n  paas: \"App Runner / Beanstalk\",\n  containers: \"ECS\",\n  k8s: \"EKS\",\n};\nconsole.log(pick);" },
    { id: 73, level: "intermediate", q: "ECR?", a: "ECR stores private images. Scan on push. Lifecycle rules. IAM on pull. CI pushes. ECS/EKS pulls.\n\nImmutable tags or digests in prod. Do not use latest as the real version. A locked pantry of labeled jars.\n\nIn the code: Allow ecr:GetDownloadUrlForLayer and BatchGetImage on the app repository ARN. That is pull permission.\n\nA common mistake is a public ECR repo for a private app. Then the image is downloadable by the world.", code: "{\n  \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n  \"Action\": [\"ecr:GetDownloadUrlForLayer\", \"ecr:BatchGetImage\"],\n  \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:ecr:ap-south-1:123:repository/app\"\n}" },
    { id: 74, level: "beginner", q: "What is CloudFormation?", a: "You write YAML or JSON stacks. CDK can generate CloudFormation from a real language. Drift detection exists.\n\nIt is verbose but first-party. Terraform is the other common answer. Both describe resources. An official blueprint form AWS already understands.\n\nIn the code: Resources Photos Type AWS::S3::Bucket with BucketName my-app-photos. That is one stack resource.\n\nA common mistake is clicking the bucket, then also declaring it in CloudFormation. The next deploy fights the click.", code: "Resources:\n  Photos:\n    Type: AWS::S3::Bucket\n    Properties:\n      BucketName: my-app-photos" },
    { id: 75, level: "intermediate", q: "CDK vs Terraform?", a: "CDK is AWS-focused, real programming language, CloudFormation underneath. Terraform is multi-cloud with its own state. Both are used a lot.\n\nPick what the team can review. State and blast radius still matter. A brand toolkit versus a universal toolkit.\n\nIn the code: wish bucket photos encrypted true. The log says CDK or Terraform can declare that wish.\n\nA common mistake is generating CDK you cannot read in PRs. Then nobody reviews the real resources.", code: "// both end as resource graphs\nconst wish = { bucket: \"photos\", encrypted: true };\nconsole.log(\"CDK or Terraform can declare\", wish);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 76, level: "intermediate", q: "Parameter Store vs Secrets Manager?", a: "Parameter Store holds config and optional SecureString, often cheaper. Secrets Manager has rotation built in, good for database passwords. Lambda reads them at runtime.\n\nDo not bake secrets in images. IAM gates GetParameter / GetSecretValue. A labeled drawer versus a safe with an auto-changing lock.\n\nIn the code: Allow secretsmanager:GetSecretValue on arn ... secret:db-*. That is read for DB secrets only.\n\nA common mistake is putting the DB password in an environment variable in the task definition YAML in git.", code: "{\n  \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n  \"Action\": [\"secretsmanager:GetSecretValue\"],\n  \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:secretsmanager:ap-south-1:123:secret:db-*\"\n}" },
    { id: 77, level: "intermediate", q: "KMS?", a: "KMS is managed encryption keys. IAM plus key policies say who may decrypt. Use it for S3, EBS, RDS, secrets.\n\nCloudTrail shows use. A key policy that is too open undoes encryption's point. Rotate as policy requires. A logged key cabinet.\n\nIn the code: Allow kms:Decrypt on a key ARN when kms:ViaService is s3 in ap-south-1. Decrypt only through S3.\n\nA common mistake is kms:* for every role. Then encryption does not limit who can read.", code: "{\n  \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n  \"Action\": [\"kms:Decrypt\"],\n  \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:kms:ap-south-1:123:key/uuid\",\n  \"Condition\": {\n    \"StringEquals\": { \"kms:ViaService\": \"s3.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com\" }\n  }\n}" },
    { id: 78, level: "intermediate", q: "WAF?", a: "WAF sits on ALB, CloudFront, or API Gateway. Rules catch SQLi-ish patterns, bots, rate limits. It is not a substitute for secure code.\n\nTune false positives. The kitchen can still poison the food. A bouncer who knows a few scam tricks.\n\nIn the code: waf on ALB, rules rate limit and common bad patterns. print shows that placement.\n\nA common mistake is WAF only, with SQL strings concatenated in the app. The bouncer cannot save a reckless kitchen.", code: "const waf = { on: \"ALB\", rules: [\"rate limit\", \"common bad patterns\"] };\nconsole.log(waf);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments\n// this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 79, level: "intermediate", q: "Shield?", a: "Shield Standard is on by default. Shield Advanced is paid, for huge attacks and a response team. Most apps never buy Advanced.\n\nArchitecture still matters: CDN, scale, no single tiny box. WAF pairs with Shield. A fence that is always there versus hiring a riot squad.\n\nIn the code: shield standard default, advanced paid retainer. print shows standard.\n\nA common mistake is buying Advanced while the app is one t2.micro with no autoscaling. The architecture is still a single point.", code: "const shield = { standard: \"default\", advanced: \"paid retainer\" };\nconsole.log(shield.standard);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments\n// this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 80, level: "intermediate", q: "GuardDuty / Security Hub / Config?", a: "GuardDuty looks for threat-ish patterns in logs. Config records resource history and rules. Security Hub aggregates findings.\n\nMention them in security interviews. They do not replace IAM hygiene. A night guard, a filing clerk, and a dashboard of both.\n\nIn the code: sec maps guardduty find threats, config record + rules, hub one inbox of findings. print shows the three.\n\nA common mistake is enabling none of them, then guessing after a strange API call.", code: "const sec = {\n  guardduty: \"find threats\",\n  config: \"record + rules\",\n  hub: \"one inbox of findings\",\n};\nconsole.log(sec);" },
    { id: 81, level: "beginner", q: "shared responsibility model?", a: "AWS secures the cloud: hardware, hypervisor, guts of managed services. You secure in the cloud: IAM, data, OS on EC2, bucket policies. The line moves up with PaaS.\n\nLambda means you do not patch OS; you still write safe code. Draw the line in interviews. A rented apartment: the building has walls; you lock your door.\n\nIn the code: split aws data centers and managed service guts, you IAM buckets app code EC2 OS. print shows both sides.\n\nA common mistake is 'AWS is secure so my public bucket is fine'. You own that policy.", code: "const split = {\n  aws: \"data centers, managed service guts\",\n  you: \"IAM, buckets, app code, EC2 OS\",\n};\nconsole.log(split);" },
    { id: 82, level: "intermediate", q: "public S3 website vs CloudFront + OAC?", a: "A public website bucket is simple and easy to misconfigure. Prefer CloudFront with Origin Access Control so the bucket stays private. OAC is the modern name to mention.\n\nSPA routing uses custom error pages to index.html. API stays elsewhere. A museum that only lets the official gift shop sell posters.\n\nIn the code: path browser -> CF -> S3 private. Comments say the bucket policy allows only the CloudFront identity.\n\nA common mistake is a public-read bucket for a React app. One wrong object ACL then leaks more than HTML.", code: "// user -> CloudFront (OAC) -> private S3\n// bucket policy allows only the CloudFront identity\nconst path = \"browser -> CF -> S3 private\";\nconsole.log(path);" },
    { id: 83, level: "intermediate", q: "S3 Transfer Acceleration / multipart?", a: "Multipart splits a big object into parts. Acceleration uses edge locations for far-away clients. Tiny JSON does not need either.\n\nSDKs often multipart for you above a size. Know they exist. Shipping a sofa in pieces, or driving to a nearby depot.\n\nIn the code: upload sizeGB 8, method multipart. print shows that choice.\n\nA common mistake is a single PUT of a 50 GB file that fails at 99%. Multipart lets you retry one part.", code: "const upload = { sizeGB: 8, method: \"multipart\" };\nconsole.log(upload);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments\n// this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 84, level: "advanced", q: "S3 performance prefix myth?", a: "Old talks said randomize prefixes for rate. After S3 scaling changes, that myth is mostly obsolete. Extremely hot single keys can still hurt at huge QPS.\n\nDo not recite 2013 advice as current. Partition only if you truly need it. An old traffic rule that the city later rebuilt.\n\nIn the code: keys are 2026/09/01/a.json and b.json. The log says date prefixes are fine for most apps.\n\nA common mistake is hashing every key 'for performance' and then being unable to list by date.", code: "const keys = [\"2026/09/01/a.json\", \"2026/09/01/b.json\"];\nconsole.log(\"date prefixes are fine for most apps\");\n// teaching snippet: read the comments\n// this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 85, level: "intermediate", q: "spot instances?", a: "Spot is cheaper and can be interrupted with a warning. Great for batch, CI, and mixed ASGs. Bad for a single irreplaceable database.\n\nStateless workers fit. Have a fallback to on-demand. Last-minute airline seats that can bump you.\n\nIn the code: asg onDemand 1, spot 4, interruptible true. print shows a mixed fleet.\n\nA common mistake is a single-node Spot database. Interruption then is an outage.", code: "const asg = { onDemand: 1, spot: 4, interruptible: true };\nconsole.log(asg);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments\n// this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 86, level: "intermediate", q: "Savings Plans / Reserved Instances?", a: "You promise 1–3 years of usage for a discount. Savings Plans are often more flexible than old RIs for compute. Finance and engineering should pick together.\n\nWrong size commitment wastes money too. Measure first. A yearly gym membership versus drop-in fees.\n\nIn the code: plan commit 1 year, type compute savings plan. print shows that commitment.\n\nA common mistake is buying RIs for a size you will not use in six months. Then you pay twice.", code: "const plan = { commit: \"1 year\", type: \"compute savings plan\" };\nconsole.log(plan);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments\n// this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 87, level: "intermediate", q: "data transfer costs?", a: "Inbound is often free. Outbound to the internet costs. Cross-AZ and NAT gateway per-GB add up. Keep traffic in-AZ and use endpoints when you can.\n\nArchitecture is a cost decision. Watch NAT. Receiving postcards is cheap; sending parcels abroad is not.\n\nIn the code: bills lists egress to internet, NAT GB, cross-AZ. print shows those silent lines.\n\nA common mistake is NAT for all S3 traffic. Endpoints would have avoided that GB charge.", code: "const bills = [\"egress to internet\", \"NAT GB\", \"cross-AZ\"];\nconsole.log(bills);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments\n// this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 88, level: "beginner", q: "What is an AZ-outage story?", a: "ALB plus ASG plus RDS Multi-AZ across two or more AZs should fail over. A single-AZ NAT or single-AZ RDS is a hidden single point of failure. Draw NAT in each AZ or use endpoints.\n\nTest failover in staging. Hope is not HA. Two substations, not one.\n\nIn the code: comments show ALB, app, NAT, RDS primary in AZ a and the matching set in AZ b. Missing NAT in b means private apps in b cannot talk out.\n\nA common mistake is Multi-AZ RDS with NAT only in AZ a. Failover then still breaks outbound.", code: "// AZ a: ALB node, app, NAT, RDS primary\n// AZ b: ALB node, app, NAT, RDS standby\n// missing NAT in b => private apps in b cannot talk out\n// teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 89, level: "advanced", q: "multi-region active-active?", a: "Data conflicts, latency, and cost make active-active hard. Active-passive with Route 53 failover and replica promotion is often enough. Do not promise active-active lightly.\n\nGlobal tables exist; they are not free of trade-offs. Start with one region well. Two kitchens cooking the same order book: whose ticket is truth?\n\nIn the code: comments show region A live, Route 53 failover, region B warm. mode is active-passive first.\n\nA common mistake is promising active-active in an interview without a conflict story. Then the design is a slogan.", code: "// active-passive\n// region A live  -> Route 53 failover -> region B warm\nconst mode = \"active-passive first\";\nconsole.log(mode);" },
    { id: 90, level: "intermediate", q: "Route 53 failover?", a: "Primary and secondary records plus health checks. Secondary might be another region. Low TTL fails over faster and costs more queries.\n\nHealth check must match user pain. DNS caches still exist. A phone that forwards if the first number does not answer.\n\nIn the code: dns primary alb-a, secondary alb-b, ttl 60. print shows that pair.\n\nA common mistake is TTL 86400 on the failover record. Clients then keep the dead address for a day.", code: "const dns = { primary: \"alb-a\", secondary: \"alb-b\", ttl: 60 };\nconsole.log(dns);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments\n// this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 91, level: "beginner", q: "What is CloudFront + S3 for a React app?", a: "Build files go to S3. CloudFront sits in front. SPA routes rewrite to index.html. The API stays on ALB or API Gateway.\n\nCache hashed assets forever. HTML can be shorter cache. OAC keeps the bucket private. A brochure rack at the edge and a kitchen in the back.\n\nIn the code: spa origin private S3, cdn CloudFront. Comments show long cache for hashed JS, short cache for index.html, SPA routes to index.html.\n\nA common mistake is caching index.html for a year. Then users keep an old app shell that points at missing hashed files.", code: "// /static/app.abc.js  long cache\n// /index.html         short cache\n// /app/route          -> index.html (SPA)\nconst spa = { origin: \"private S3\", cdn: \"CloudFront\" };\nconsole.log(spa);" },
    { id: 92, level: "intermediate", q: "Cognito?", a: "User pools handle sign-up and login. Identity pools can hand AWS credentials to users. Some teams still use Auth0, Firebase, or custom JWT.\n\nStill design session expiry and MFA. Fine for many apps. A hotel that checks passports and then issues room keys.\n\nIn the code: handler reads JWT claims sub from API Gateway authorizer. Returns that sub as JSON.\n\nA common mistake is putting Cognito tokens in localStorage on a XSS-prone page. Then one script steals the session.", code: "exports.handler = async (event) => {\n  const sub = event.requestContext?.authorizer?.jwt?.claims?.sub;\n  return { statusCode: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ sub }) };\n};" },
    { id: 93, level: "intermediate", q: "Step Functions?", a: "You orchestrate Lambda and other services with retries, parallel, wait, and approval. Prefer it over a spaghetti of chained functions. Keep each Lambda small.\n\nErrors belong in the state machine. Visual workflows help reviews. A recipe card the kitchen must follow, including wait 10 minutes.\n\nIn the code: machine is validate, charge, email, done. join prints that order.\n\nA common mistake is Lambda A directly invoking B, C, and D with home-grown retries. Then you cannot see the workflow.", code: "const machine = [\"validate\", \"charge\", \"email\", \"done\"];\nconsole.log(machine.join(\" -> \"));\n// teaching snippet: read the comments\n// this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 94, level: "intermediate", q: "Glue / Athena / Redshift at a glance?", a: "Glue is ETL. Athena is SQL on S3, pay per scan. Redshift is a warehouse. Not OLTP for the live app.\n\nApp traffic stays on RDS or Dynamo. Scan less data in Athena with partitions. Factory, query window on files, dedicated warehouse.\n\nIn the code: analytics files S3, sql Athena, warehouse Redshift. print shows the three toys.\n\nA common mistake is Athena on the live checkout path. Scan-priced SQL is not your OLTP database.", code: "const analytics = { files: \"S3\", sql: \"Athena\", warehouse: \"Redshift\" };\nconsole.log(analytics);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments\n// this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 95, level: "intermediate", q: "CloudWatch alarm to action?", a: "Alarm goes to SNS, then email, Slack, PagerDuty, or autoscaling. An alarm with no action is a pretty graph. Page on user pain.\n\nInclude a runbook link in the alarm description. A smoke alarm wired to a bell, not a painting of a bell.\n\nIn the code: alarm metric 5xx, action sns-topic-oncall. print shows that wiring.\n\nA common mistake is 50 alarms to email that nobody reads. Then the real 5xx is noise.", code: "const alarm = { metric: \"5xx\", action: \"sns-topic-oncall\" };\nconsole.log(alarm);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments\n// this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 96, level: "beginner", q: "How do you keep AWS costs down?", a: "Stop idle EC2. Prefer gp3. Lifecycle S3. Rightsize. Spot for batch. Kill unused NAT and load balancers. Use budgets.\n\nNAT and idle LBs are classic leaks. Tags tell you who owns the waste. Review weekly at first. Turning off lights and not renting empty rooms.\n\nIn the code: waste is idle EC2, unused NAT, unlifecycle S3. print shows those leaks.\n\nA common mistake is a NAT Gateway left from a tutorial. It bills every hour even with no traffic.", code: "const waste = [\"idle EC2\", \"unused NAT\", \"unlifecycle S3\"];\nconsole.log(waste);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments\n// this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 97, level: "intermediate", q: "AWS Organizations / SCP?", a: "Prod, dev, security, log-archive accounts. SCPs are ceilings no child can exceed. Control Tower / Landing Zone builds the skeleton.\n\nA leaked dev key should not list prod buckets. SSO into roles. A parent lock on the whole house, not one bedroom rule.\n\nIn the code: Deny * unless aws:RequestedRegion is ap-south-1. That SCP blocks other regions for the children.\n\nA common mistake is one account for everything. Then a leaked intern key sees prod.", code: "{\n  \"Effect\": \"Deny\",\n  \"Action\": \"*\",\n  \"Resource\": \"*\",\n  \"Condition\": {\n    \"StringNotEquals\": { \"aws:RequestedRegion\": [\"ap-south-1\"] }\n  }\n}" },
    { id: 98, level: "advanced", q: "why multiple accounts?", a: "Blast radius, bills, IAM mistakes, prod isolation. A leaked dev key should not see prod. More accounts means more plumbing: SSO, networking.\n\nLog archive account is a common pattern. Start with a few, not a hundred. Separate companies under one parent, not one shared junk drawer.\n\nIn the code: orgs is prod, dev, security, logs. print shows that split.\n\nA common mistake is 80 accounts on day one with no SSO. Then login is the outage.", code: "const orgs = [\"prod\", \"dev\", \"security\", \"logs\"];\nconsole.log(orgs);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments\n// this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 99, level: "intermediate", q: "Well-Architected review?", a: "Operational excellence, security, reliability, performance, cost, sustainability. Even without an official review, it is a good interview structure. You do not need a certificate to talk in pillars.\n\nPick one risk per pillar. Be honest about gaps. A health checkup with named sections.\n\nIn the code: pillars lists ops, security, reliability, performance, cost, sustainability. print shows all six.\n\nA common mistake is only talking cost. Then security and reliability never enter the sketch.", code: "const pillars = [\n  \"ops\", \"security\", \"reliability\",\n  \"performance\", \"cost\", \"sustainability\",\n];\nconsole.log(pillars);" },
    { id: 100, level: "advanced", q: "How would you draw a standard web app on AWS?", a: "Users hit Route 53, then CloudFront, then an ALB in public subnets. Apps sit in private ASG or ECS. RDS Multi-AZ and cache in private subnets. S3 for files.\n\nIAM on every arrow. CloudWatch and CloudTrail on. Mention Multi-AZ NAT or endpoints. Say Multi-AZ out loud. A campus map you can sketch in two minutes.\n\nIn the code: comments draw R53 to CloudFront to ALB public, ALB to private app, app to RDS Multi-AZ plus Redis, app to S3 with an IAM role. logs CloudWatch, audit CloudTrail.\n\nA common mistake is drawing only boxes with no IAM or AZ story. Then the sketch is a poster, not a design.", code: "// users -> R53 -> CloudFront -> ALB (public)\n// ALB -> app (private ASG/ECS)\n// app -> RDS Multi-AZ + Redis (private)\n// app -> S3 (IAM role, not keys)\n// logs: CloudWatch   audit: CloudTrail" }
  ]
};
